<h1>Drill: Generate, Validate, Retry</h1>

<div class="q"><h3>1. One artifact, two audiences</h3>
<p>The validator's error strings serve two consumers. Name them and give
the design consequence.</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>The human (editor toast/CLI output) and the MODEL (retry feedback,
verbatim). Consequence: errors must be specific and actionable —
"unknown part: hair_dreads (nearest valid id: hair_braids)" — because a
vague error wastes a retry exactly like it wastes a player's time.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>2. All three models failed identically. What did that prove?</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>That the CONTRACT was at fault, not the models. Multi-line persona text
inside JSON strings is awkward to emit; switching to one JSON object per
character (converted server-side so the existing loaders stay the only
loaders) cleared the failures across every model at once.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>3. The unsatisfiable instruction</h3>
<p>"Keep looks style-consistent" kept failing until one vocabulary change.
What was missing, and what's the general principle?</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>The part list never said which family each id belonged to — the model
couldn't comply even in principle. Annotating <code>body_round(round)</code>
etc. took qwen3:8b to first-attempt validity. Principle: before repeating
an instruction louder, check whether the prompt contains the INFORMATION
the instruction requires.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>4. Why is auto-repair banned even for obvious fixes?</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>Nudging placements or substituting nearest ids hides model quality
(poisoning the benchmark that picks the model), ships silent surprises,
and removes the feedback pressure that makes retries improve. The one
exception is wrapper normalization ("[grumpy]" → grumpy): packaging, not
content.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>5. Repairable vs strict loads</h3>
<p>Hand-made maps load repairably; LLM output loads strictly. Justify the
asymmetry.</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>A human editing their own file benefits from "open it anyway, drop the
bad rows, show a toast" — they can fix it in place. A model gets feedback
instead of forgiveness: strictness keeps validity measurable and pushes
errors back into the retry loop where they can be corrected.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>6. Why does the Python bench shell out to the C++ CLI?</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>So the bench and the game share ONE validation contract. A Python
re-implementation would drift, and then the bench would measure compliance
with rules the game doesn't actually enforce.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>7. Transfer it</h3>
<p>You're adding "LLM writes SQL migrations" to a tool. Apply tonight's
architecture in three bullets.</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>(1) The model emits a constrained spec (schema-change JSON against the
live schema as closed-world vocabulary), never raw SQL applied directly.
(2) A validator with model-grade errors gates the ONE apply path; failures
feed back verbatim, capped, no partial applies. (3) Model choice by
measured spec-validity over a fixed corpus, validated by the same code
that gates production.</p>
</div></details></div>
